---

*Reference: [CLI](cli.tmd) · [Shell completion](shell-completion.tmd) · [Front matter](frontmatter.tmd) · [Cell options](cell-options.tmd) · Configuration · [Shortcodes](shortcodes.tmd) · [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.tmd) · [Accessibility](accessibility.tmd) · [Licensing](licensing.tmd)*

Two places hold configuration: a document's **YAML front matter** (the `---`
block at the top), and a project's **`_site.yml`** (for a site or book). The
project config uses a flat, native schema. A nested config (a
`project:` / `website:` / `book:` / `format:` block) is no longer parsed: its
unknown top-level keys warn, and its nested values are ignored. Flatten it (see
the note at the end).

This page is the project (`_site.yml`) and page-block (`listing:`)
reference. For the full per-key catalogue of a document's front matter, see
[Front matter](frontmatter.tmd); for cell options and shortcodes, see
[Cell options](cell-options.tmd) and [Shortcodes](shortcodes.tmd). The summary
table below repeats the front-matter keys most often paired with project config.

## Document front matter

| Key | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `title`, `subtitle`, `author`, `date`, `description` | Metadata; `title` drives `<title>`, the title block, OpenGraph, and nav/listing labels |
| `format` | `html` (default), `deck`, or a `<name>-deck` variant |
| `theme` | `light` / `dark`, a `.css`/`.scss` file, or an installed `_extensions/<name>/` bundle. Only an explicit `light` (or `default`) / `dark` forces that mode; unset (or a custom theme) follows the reader's OS `prefers-color-scheme`, falling back to light |
| `toc` | Forces the table of contents on or off for this page; leave it out and it is automatic (an article with enough headings gets one) |
| `title-block-style` | `none` keeps the title metadata but hides the visible `<h1>` header |
| `bibliography` | A `.bib` file enabling `[@key]` citations + an auto References section (rendered in a single built-in IEEE style) |
| `page-layout` | `full` widens the content column (the full page width, good for listing indexes and wide tables/figures); omit it for the default centered reading width set by `--tali-maxw` |
| `lang` | BCP-47 language tag for `<html lang>` (default `en`) |
| `csl` | **Recognized, but not supported: `check` warns and the key does nothing.** References always render in the built-in IEEE style. It stays recognized so an author carrying it over from another tool is told the truth rather than nothing; delete it |
| `categories`, `image`, `image-alt` | `categories`/`image` feed `listing:` cards + social meta; set `image-alt` whenever you set `image:` (an `image:` with no `image-alt:` warns; use `image-alt: ""` to declare it decorative) |
| `draft` | `true` keeps the page (or book chapter) out of a `build`/`publish` (output, nav, listings) **and out of `check`**, which names what it held back; `preview` still shows it, badged, and lints it |

### Code-cell options

Inside a cell, lines beginning `#|` (`//|` for `{js}`, `%%|` for `{mermaid}`)
set per-cell options:

| Option | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `echo: false` | Hide the source, show only the output |
| `code-fold: true` + `code-summary: "…"` | Collapse the listing behind a summary |
| `label: fig-… ` + `fig-cap: "…"` | Make the output a numbered, cross-referenceable figure |
| `include: false` | Run the cell but drop its output |

A front-matter `execute:` block sets document-wide `echo`/`include`/`cache` defaults
that a cell's own `#|` options override.

## Project `_site.yml`

A **directory** with a `_site.yml` is a site or book project. The schema is
flat: Taliesin is HTML-only, so there's no `format: html:` nesting, and the
project type is inferred (presence of `chapters:` ⇒ a book). There is no
`_metadata.yml` cascade: the root config sets defaults, a page's own front matter
overrides them.

A **website**:

```yaml
title: "My Site"
description: "Notes on things"
url: "https://example.com"
favicon: favicon.svg
logo: logo.svg           # brand image in the navbar (book: the topbar + chapter drawer)
head: head.html          # raw markup for every page's <head> — the one escape hatch
bibliography: refs.bib   # one .bib for every page (see below)

nav:                     # a list is the left side; or { left: [...], right: [...] }
  - { text: Blog,     href: blog.tmd }
  - { text: Projects, href: projects.tmd }

footer:                  # a string is left text; or { left, center, right }
  left: "© 2026 Me"
  right:
    - { icon: github, href: "https://github.com/me" }
```

A **book**: just add `chapters:` (its presence makes it a book; no `type:`):

```yaml
title: "My Book"
author: "Me"
toc: true
chapters:
  - index.tmd
  - part: "Part One"     # group chapters under a part header
    chapters: [a.tmd, b.tmd]
  - file: c.tmd          # { file:, text: } overrides the chapter label
    text: "Conclusions"
```

A chapter entry is a bare path (`- a.tmd`), a `{ part:, chapters: }` group header, or
a `{ file:, text: }` mapping whose `text:` overrides the chapter label (the override
also works inside a part's list). Without `text:`, the label is the chapter's first
`# H1`, then its front-matter `title:`, then the file stem.

| Key | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `title`, `author`, `description`, `url` | Project metadata (`title` is the navbar brand / book title) |
| `favicon` | Tab icon (defaults to the Taliesin mark) |
| `logo` | Brand image, replacing the wordmark in the navbar (a website) or the topbar **and** the chapter drawer (a book). Project-relative like `favicon`; `title` becomes its `alt`. The same key a deck's front matter takes, so one name brands all three surfaces. There are no size or position sub-keys: the stylesheet caps it against the bar it sits in |
| `image` | Deprecated; the social-card image is now auto-generated per page |
| `head` | Raw markup injected into every page's `<head>`: a path, a `{ text: … }` / `{ file: … }` map, or a list of those. The **one** raw-injection hatch — analytics, a search-console `<meta>`, a custom stylesheet |
| `nav` | Navbar items: a list (left), or `{ left, right }` |
| `footer` | Footer: a string, or `{ left, center, right }` of items |
| `chapters` | Book chapter order (a file, `{ file, text }`, or `{ part, chapters }`); presence ⇒ a book |
| `mounts` | Mount other Taliesin projects under a URL prefix: a map `{ docs: ../docs }`. `preview` serves each live; `build` writes each into `<out>/<at>/`, so the links resolve in the deploy too. The path is relative to the site root and may climb at most one level, so a mount reaches a project *beside* this one and nowhere else; an absolute or further-climbing path is ignored with a warning |
| `bibliography` | A `.bib` shared by every page of the project (a path, or a list of them), relative to `_site.yml`. Merged **under** each page's own `bibliography:`, so a page can cite a shared key and still add or correct entries locally |
| `python`, `r` | Pin the interpreter this project runs code cells against (see *Interpreter selection* below). A path or command name; the highest-precedence source, so a committed project is reproducible regardless of the ambient shell |

**A shared bibliography.** `bibliography:` in `_site.yml` is read by every page, so a
series of posts citing one reference file declares it once instead of repeating the
line in each front matter. It is a *layer*, not a fallback: a page that also declares
its own `bibliography:` gets both, with the page's entry winning on a shared key — the
way to correct one reference for one post without editing the file every other page
reads.

Two read-only checks come with it, reported against `_site.yml` by `taliesin check`
and `build`. A **duplicate key** inside the shared file would otherwise silently
last-write-win. An entry **no page cites** is dead weight; that one is site-wide by
necessity, since a shared entry one page cites is in use however many pages leave it
alone. It is advice rather than an error, so it never fails a build unless you ask
with `--strict`. A page's *own* `bibliography:` gets the same never-cited check scoped
to that page. Neither check ever edits a `.bib`.

**Interpreter selection.** A `{python}` cell needs a Python with `ipykernel`, an
`{r}` cell an R with `IRkernel`. Taliesin picks each interpreter by the first source
that exists, so a project can pin its own without fighting your global shell:

1. the `_site.yml` `python:` / `r:` field;
2. a project-local `.venv` (`<project>/.venv/bin/python`, then `.venv/bin/python3`); Python only, no R convention;
3. the `TALIESIN_PYTHON` / `TALIESIN_R` environment variable;
4. the bare `python3` / `R` on your `PATH`.

Because a project `.venv` (or a `python:` pin) beats a global `TALIESIN_PYTHON`, a
committed project reproduces the same interpreter on any machine. The chosen
interpreter and where it came from are logged the first time each language's kernel
starts (`python -> /proj/.venv/bin/python  (from .venv)`), and `taliesin check` prints
an **Environment** section listing it plus whether its Jupyter kernel package is
importable. A single `.tmd` with no `_site.yml` uses the same order minus the field
(`.venv` beside the file, then env, then default).

**Icons.** A nav/footer item can be `{ icon: <name>, href: … }` instead of raw
HTML; bundled glyphs: `github`, `linkedin`, `rss`, `x`, `mastodon`, `bluesky`,
`email`.

**Table of contents.** A site-wide `toc: true` shows the right-hand "on this page"
list only on pages with **at least three headings**, so a short page doesn't get a
near-empty rail. To override the auto-gate, set `toc:` in a page's own front matter:
an explicit `toc: true` / `toc: false` always wins. On narrow screens the list becomes
a pull-up sheet (a handle at the bottom of the screen: tap or drag it up) instead of a
sidebar.

`toc:` is a **website** key. A book has no right-hand rail: its in-chapter outline is
the Chapters drawer, which expands the chapter you are reading down to its
sub-subsections, so a second, shallower list beside the text earned nothing. Writing
`toc:` in a book's `_site.yml` warns and does nothing; delete the key.

**Validation.** Unrecognized keys are reported with a "did you mean …" hint, offered
when a known key is within edit distance 2, so a typo like `favicn:` doesn't silently do
nothing. Validation only **warns**: the document still renders, and every warning is
**located**, so in the live preview you can click it straight to the source line. The same
closed-vocabulary check covers unknown cell `#|` options, unknown immediate children of
`execute:` / `listing:` / `hero:`, and `format:` sub-keys (nothing reads those, so a
green check must not certify them). (The Internals book covers the design,
and the drift-locked Draft 2020-12 schemas behind the `taliesin schema` command, in its
*Validation & schemas* chapter.)

## Installing a site as an app

Every built site and book ships a `manifest.webmanifest`, so a reader can install it and
come back to it without hunting for a bookmark:

| Where | How it appears |
|---|---|
| Chrome / Edge, desktop and Android | An **Install** button in the address bar; the site opens in its own window with a taskbar or launcher icon |
| iOS / iPadOS | Share, then **Add to Home Screen**. Manual only: Safari never prompts |
| macOS Safari | **Add to Dock** |
| Firefox on desktop | Manifests are ignored. Nothing breaks; there is simply no install button |

Installing changes how a reader *returns* to your site. It does **not** make the site work
offline. For offline reading, a book build also emits a `<book>.zip` that readers download
from the topbar and keep.

Nothing to configure: the app name comes from `title:`, the label from the part of it
before the first colon ("Taliesin: The User Guide" becomes "Taliesin"), and the scope from
wherever the site is deployed, so a book published under `/docs/guide/` installs as its own
app.

### Bringing your own icon

By default an installed site wears the Taliesin mark. To use your own, drop two PNGs next
to `_site.yml`:

```
icon-192.png            192 x 192
icon-512.png            512 x 512
icon-maskable-512.png   512 x 512, optional
```

Both of the first two are required. With only one, the built-in mark is used instead,
because Chrome will not offer to install at all unless both sizes are present, and a
half-replaced icon set would mix your mark with Taliesin's.

The optional maskable icon is the one Android crops to a circle, so keep its artwork inside
the middle 80%. Make every image square and fully opaque: transparent corners render black
on an iOS home screen.

`favicon:` is separate and still controls the browser tab icon. It can stay an SVG;
manifests do not accept SVG, which is why the app icons are their own files.

## Editor autocomplete (config schema)

Taliesin ships JSON Schemas for both config surfaces so your editor's YAML
language server can autocomplete keys, show hovers, and flag unknown keys as you
type, before you render. There is no Taliesin language server to install: the
schemas describe the same key sets Taliesin validates against at render time, so
the editor and the renderer always agree.

Drop the schemas into your project:

```sh
taliesin schema --out .tmd
```

Then point the YAML language server at them with a modeline.

For `_site.yml` (a plain YAML file, works out of the box), add at the very top:

```yaml
# yaml-language-server: $schema=.tmd/tali-site.schema.json
title: My site
```

For a document's front matter, put the modeline inside the `---` block:

```yaml
---
# yaml-language-server: $schema=.tmd/tali-frontmatter.schema.json
title: My post
---
```

Front-matter validation needs an editor that treats a `.tmd` file's `---` block
as embedded YAML (for example the VS Code YAML extension). A plain YAML language
server does not parse a `.tmd` file as YAML, so the front-matter modeline only
takes effect with that support; `_site.yml` needs no such setup.

### Page blocks (sites)

Two front-matter blocks turn a page into something special: `listing:` (an
index of other pages) and `hero:` (a landing banner). `listing:` is detailed
below; `hero:` is documented in [Front matter](frontmatter.tmd) and shown in
context in [Formats](../using/formats.tmd).

#### `listing:` (an index page)

A `listing:` block fills the page with one card per document found under
`contents:`, newest-first by default:

```yaml
listing:
  contents: posts          # directory to crawl (relative to this page); required
  type: grid               # grid (cards) | list (rows + thumbnail) | default (text rows)
  max-items: 10            # cap the number of cards (omit for all)
  id: recent               # optional: fill a `::: {#recent}` block instead of appending
```

| Key | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `contents` | Directory to crawl, relative to the index page. Every `.tmd` under it with a `title:` becomes a card. Required |
| `type` | `grid` tiles the cards with their images; `list` is a reading-first stacked layout that keeps each card's image as a thumbnail beside the text; `default` (the fallback) is the same stacked layout without images, for a formal text list. Both `grid` and `list` show the `image:` thumbnail; `default` does not |
| `max-items` | Cap the number of cards shown; omit it to list every match |
| `id` | Fill an existing `::: {#id}` container with the listing instead of appending it to the page, so you can place prose around it |

A card's date, categories, and image come from each listed page's own front
matter. To give a page a category badge on its card, set `categories:` on
**that page**:

```yaml
---
title: "A post about rust"
date: 2026-06-20
categories: [rust, performance]   # these become the card's badges
image: cover.png                  # card thumbnail in a `type: grid` or `type: list` listing
image-alt: "A flame graph of the hot loop"   # required whenever you set `image:`
---
```

## Project structure & reserved names

A multi-page project is just a directory with a `_site.yml`. Taliesin discovers the
pages by walking that directory, with one rule: **anything whose name starts with
`_` or `.` is not a page.** That single convention is what keeps config, partials,
caches, and build output from turning into navigable pages.

```text
my-site/
├── _site.yml            # project config; presence makes this a site/book
├── index.tmd            # a page (the home page)
├── about.tmd            # a page
├── posts/
│   └── first.tmd        # a page (nested pages are discovered too)
├── _includes/           # `_`-prefixed ⇒ skipped: partials for {{< include >}}
│   └── _disclaimer.tmd
├── _extensions/         # `_`-prefixed ⇒ skipped: reusable theme bundles
│   └── my-theme/
├── _freeze/             # `_`-prefixed ⇒ skipped: execution cache (gitignored)
└── _site/               # build output (_book/ for a book; elsewhere with build --out)
```

Reserved names, all skipped by page discovery:

| Name | What it is |
|------|------------|
| `_site.yml` | the project config (its presence is what defines a project) |
| `_includes/` | a convention for `{{< include >}}` partials; the `_` keeps them out of the page set (the leading `_` on the partial files themselves, e.g. `_intro.tmd`, is also enough) |
| `_extensions/` | reusable theme bundles: a `_extensions/<name>/theme.css` selected by name from `theme:` |
| `_freeze/` | the persistent execution cache, keyed by content hash; gitignore it |
| `_site/` / `_book/` | build output (`build <dir>` writes here; write elsewhere with `build --out <dir>`) |
| anything `.`-prefixed | hidden files/dirs (`.git`, …) are skipped too |

A `404.tmd` is special-cased the other way: it builds to `404.html` at the site root
(most static hosts serve it for unknown URLs) but stays out of the nav.

### How a deck gets built

A slide deck is any doc with `format: deck`. How it ends up in your output
depends on *how the project refers to it*: there are three cases, and missing the
distinction is the usual way a deck goes missing from a build:

- **Standalone**: `taliesin preview deck.tmd` or `taliesin build deck.tmd`. The deck
  is the whole document; it renders as slides. Nothing else needed.
- **Embedded**: `{{< embed deck.tmd >}}` from a page or chapter. The deck is built as
  its own standalone slide file and shown inline as an isolating iframe; it is kept
  *out* of the nav and search (it isn't a page, it's an inclusion). This is how to put
  a deck inside a site or book. See [Formats](../using/formats.tmd).
- **Loose (warns)**: a `format: deck` file sitting in a site directory that no
  page embeds and that isn't a standalone target. It is neither chaptered nor embedded,
  so the build has no reason to emit it; Taliesin warns and would otherwise flatten it
  into a chrome-wrapped article. **Fix:** embed it from a page, or build it standalone.

